We study the geometry and \(d\)-dimensional volume of the stepping-stone
diversion region in Euclidean space. Its volume factorizes as the \(d\)th
power of the distance between the defining sites times a normalized constant
\(a_{d,\mathrm{SS}}(\alpha)\). We derive an explicit boundary
parametrization that reduces the original \(d\)-dimensional volume problem
to a one-dimensional integral. We recover the degenerate and \tb{Gabriel
cases and show that, after removing the two defining sites, the
finite-parameter exclusion regions converge from within to the open
relative-neighborhood lune}. The limiting volume is
expressed through the regularized incomplete beta function. We prove strict region and volume
monotonicity in \(\alpha\), and use the resulting inclusions to place the
induced graphs among classical proximity graphs. We apply Devroye's
expected-size framework to obtain the asymptotic expected degree of the
stepping-stone graph in terms of \(a_{d,\mathrm{SS}}(\alpha)\). In the
planar Poisson model considered by Watanabe, we further show that the
normalized area determines the corresponding restricted-search edge-length
density.
Finally, we derive a numerically stable quadrature formula and verify it
independently in 18 rejection-sampling experiments, each with \(10^8\)
samples and with every discrepancy below two Monte Carlo standard errors.
